It's going to build the image.

260
00:10:38,560 --> 00:10:42,690
Then it's going to
boot your QEMU image.

261
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:48,830
What you should see
here shortly after

262
00:10:48,830 --> 00:10:51,965
it boots the QEMU image,

263
00:10:51,965 --> 00:10:55,115
it's going to login over SSH.

264
00:10:55,115 --> 00:10:58,560
Using that dropbear SSH client.

265
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:10,250
Then it's waiting a minute

266
00:11:10,250 --> 00:11:13,710
to ensure that all
processes are started up.

267
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:20,815
Then you should see it
run your test script

268
00:11:20,815 --> 00:11:23,240
from inside QEMU.

269
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:26,635
The results should match

270
00:11:26,635 --> 00:11:28,650
what you would expect
if you run it natively.

271
00:11:28,650 --> 00:11:31,970
That your finder script,

272
00:11:31,970 --> 00:11:33,770
your brighter
application, all work

273
00:11:33,770 --> 00:11:36,410
successfully when
run inside QEMU.

274
00:11:36,410 --> 00:11:39,245
If you see this
last message here,

275
00:11:39,245 --> 00:11:40,925
you should see that it

276
00:11:40,925 --> 00:11:43,160
completed your tests
with a success.

277
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:47,285
You can also run the
components interactively.

278
00:11:47,285 --> 00:11:49,940
If you look at the content
of this directory,

279
00:11:49,940 --> 00:11:51,920
you should see a run QEMU script

280
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:54,170
that you can use to run
QEMU independently.

281
00:11:54,170 --> 00:11:57,260
If I run QEMU script,

282
00:11:57,260 --> 00:12:01,400
this is going to bring
me up to a login prompt.

283
00:12:01,400 --> 00:12:06,890
Now because I've set my root
password in a buildroot,

284
00:12:06,890 --> 00:12:10,325
I can log in here as root, root.

285
00:12:10,325 --> 00:12:13,920
Now I can just run find a test.

286
00:12:16,660 --> 00:12:19,310
It should pick out my
finder test and it

287
00:12:19,310 --> 00:12:21,260
should run the test
independently.

288
00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:22,160
The reason all this is

289
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:23,480
working is because
we're putting it

290
00:12:23,480 --> 00:12:26,820
in a directory that
includes the path.

291
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:30,880
Then to exit, I
can do Control A,

292
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:34,255
C, and Q to exit QEMU.

293
00:12:34,255 --> 00:12:36,400
Second thing you'll
notice here about

294
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:37,855
the run QEMU script,

295
00:12:37,855 --> 00:12:39,810
I just look at the content.

296
00:12:39,810 --> 00:12:42,170
You can see here this
line that's saying

297
00:12:42,170 --> 00:12:45,275
hostfwd 1022 to 22.

298
00:12:45,275 --> 00:12:46,400
What this is going to do is take

299
00:12:46,400 --> 00:12:48,770
the local host port 1022,

300
00:12:48,770 --> 00:12:51,200
and forward that into
port 22 in the container.

301
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:53,015
Twenty-two is our SSH port.

302
00:12:53,015 --> 00:12:55,039
That's going to
allow us to access

303
00:12:55,039 --> 00:12:59,330
the QEMU virtual machine from
the host using port 1022.

304
00:12:59,330 --> 00:13:01,610
Let me show you how that works.

305
00:13:01,610 --> 00:13:04,100
If I do a run QEMU in

306
00:13:04,100 --> 00:13:07,980
this window and I start
my QEMU instance,

307
00:13:13,150 --> 00:13:15,710
and once it's boot up,

308
00:13:15,710 --> 00:13:19,490
I can take a new terminal
window on the host and run

309
00:13:19,490 --> 00:13:23,660
my SSH command to
SSH in on port 22.

310
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:25,985
Make that a little bigger
so it's easy to see.

311
00:13:25,985 --> 00:13:28,490
You can see here that
I'm going to SSH

312
00:13:28,490 --> 00:13:32,360
in the dash p argument
gives a port, so port 1022.

313
00:13:32,360 --> 00:13:33,920
I'm going to use username root,

314
00:13:33,920 --> 00:13:37,550
and I'm using localhost from
the host build machine.

315
00:13:37,550 --> 00:13:39,740
This is just doing
a loop back to

316
00:13:39,740 --> 00:13:41,945
my exact same IP
address, my host.

317
00:13:41,945 --> 00:13:44,810
But because I've
port forwarded 1022,

318
00:13:44,810 --> 00:13:48,590
I'm going to be logging into
my QEMU instance over SSH.

319
00:13:48,590 --> 00:13:51,290
I do this, I'll get
prompted for a password,

320
00:13:51,290 --> 00:13:52,700
enter the root password,

321
00:13:52,700 --> 00:13:59,840
I'll be inside the QEMU

322
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:03,290
running instance of
my embedded system.

323
00:14:03,290 --> 00:14:06,485
You can see I can run my
finder tests then from SSH,

324
00:14:06,485 --> 00:14:08,660
everything works just like
it would if I had logged in

325
00:14:08,660 --> 00:14:12,660
interactively through
the QEMU script.

326
00:14:13,030 --> 00:14:17,195
One benefit of this
implementation

327
00:14:17,195 --> 00:14:20,270
is that we can use
this with SCP.

328
00:14:20,270 --> 00:14:26,795
If I want to pull a file
off of the raw file system,

329
00:14:26,795 --> 00:14:28,760
I could do SCP.

330
00:14:28,760 --> 00:14:31,865
I need to use a capital
dash p for port.

331
00:14:31,865 --> 00:14:35,015
But I can say root@localhost
and then I can use

332
00:14:35,015 --> 00:14:39,455
user then finder test.sh.

333
00:14:39,455 --> 00:14:42,500
Let's just copy that
into my local directory.

334
00:14:42,500 --> 00:14:45,065
It prompted for a
password again.

335
00:14:45,065 --> 00:14:49,800
There you can see a copies
that file in locally.

336
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,100
This should match what was
running on the device.

337
00:14:54,100 --> 00:14:56,110
This gives you some
useful tools you

338
00:14:56,110 --> 00:14:57,955
can use for interactively
working with

339
00:14:57,955 --> 00:15:02,480
the QEMU running image
of your instance.